WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.455 --> 00:00:02.213
- One warning, that's it.

00:00:02.213 --> 00:00:03.220
You gave me one.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:05.517
- [Reporter] You may have seen
the videos on social media.

00:00:05.517 --> 00:00:07.400
(people shouting)

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.099
Or experienced it firsthand.

00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:11.040
(people grumbling)

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.450
According to airlines,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.680
the Association of Flight
Attendants-CWA union,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.970
and the FAA, disruptive,
unruly and violent passengers

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.910
have become a serious problem.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.040
Last year, the FAA reported
a record high number

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.200
of unruly passenger reports.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.590
Although incidents have fallen

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.430
since spiking earlier in the year,

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.920
today's rate remains
above pre-pandemic levels.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.420
- This past year of starting
at the beginning of 2021

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.830
has been the most difficult

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.550
and consistently dangerous
time in our careers.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.550
We have been kicked, punched, spit at,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.599
had trash thrown at us,
and it's taken its toll.

00:00:51.599 --> 00:00:53.560
- [Reporter] In a survey taken last summer

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.140
of 5,000 flight attendants
from 30 airlines,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.880
85% said they had dealt with
unruly passengers in 2021,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.160
with over half saying they'd experienced

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.270
five or more such incidents

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.700
and 17% saying they were involved

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.260
in an incident that got physical.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.630
One solution that's gaining traction

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.420
is a federal no-fly list.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.070
- Unruly passengers who've been convicted

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:17.990
of in-flight offenses

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.940
that they'd be put on
a special no-fly list

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.700
and not be allowed to fly on any airline.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.210
- [Reporter] According to the FAA,

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.250
70% of last year's passenger reports

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.470
were related to mask related incidents.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:34.870
So far this year, 65% of
incidents are related to masks.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:38.180
Now, with in flight and
airport mask mandates

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.110
slated to be lifted in March,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.593
is a national no-fly list really needed?

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.910
Earlier this year, the
CEO of Delta Airlines

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.060
Ed Bastian wrote to the
US Justice Department,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.220
asking the agency to create
a national no-fly list

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.710
for anyone convicted
of disrupting a flight.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.370
Bastian wrote, "A no-fly
list of unruly passengers

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.230
will help prevent future incidents

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:05.520
and serve as a strong
symbol of the consequences

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.860
of not complying with
crew member instructions

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.680
on commercial aircraft."

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.850
- All the airlines over
the last year or two

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.180
as these conflicts in flights

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.970
and disputes over masks have ramped up,

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.400
they've been administering
their own lists of people

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.420
that they won't have on board anymore.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.070
- [Reporter] Delta said it
had put nearly 1900 people

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.460
on its list for refusing to
comply with masking requirements

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.220
and submitted more than 900 ban names

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.110
to the TSA to pursue civil penalties.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.323
- The concern is so that they don't

00:02:34.323 --> 00:02:36.480
and really can't share that information.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.140
So somebody that has caused
a problem on a United Flight

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.100
and they can no longer fly United

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.920
because of the airlines' policies

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.890
on theory, they can book
their next flight on Delta

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.050
where they may end up
being disrupted again.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.980
- [Reporter] Delta said
that while such incidents

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.590
of bad behavior represent
just a small fraction

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.000
of the overall flights on Delta,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.840
the rate of incidents on the airline

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.360
has increased nearly 100% since 2019.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.060
- Flight attendants are
absolutely not willing

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.560
to accept this as the new normal.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.820
So what they're saying is make it stop.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.420
You also see this in the
fact that across the board,

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.920
the flight attendants are
just not willing to pick up

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.480
the voluntary overtime hours

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.230
that they were before the pandemic.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.040
And so the public saw that

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.460
in many flight cancellations in the fall

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.570
and even over the holidays.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.560
- [Reporter] In 2021,
the FAA said it initiated

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.960
nearly 1100 investigations
into unruly passengers,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.370
which is more than the
previous seven years combined.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.050
- Flight attendants will
say a lot of these incidents

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.580
don't necessarily have
anything to do with masks.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.240
Some of them involve alcohol,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.918
and some of them just seem
to defy all explanation.

00:03:40.918 --> 00:03:44.050
(people shouting)

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.320
- [Reporter] The Biden administration

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.000
has voiced its support for the idea.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.883
- Anything besides zero,
is an unacceptable rate.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.130
- [Reporter] But not everyone is in favor

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.200
of a national no-fly list.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.070
Recently, a group of Republican senators

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.250
wrote a letter to Attorney
General Merrick Garland,

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.690
suggesting that such a law
could infringe on civil rights.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.250
- The Republican senators
who opposed the letter,

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:08.900
basically they argued that it would give

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.320
the Department Of Justice

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.440
too much authority to create a list,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.450
and there wouldn't
necessarily be transparency

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.080
and clarity into who would be on the list,

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:19.050
and that this really is something

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.520
that should come through Congress.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.597
- [Reporter] The senators wrote,

00:04:21.597 --> 00:04:24.460
"The creation of this list by DOJ

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.500
would result in a severe restriction

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:27.840
on the ability of citizens

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.840
to fully exercise their
constitutional right

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.260
to engage in interstate transportation."

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.170
The senators also noted that
since most of last year's

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.240
unruly passenger incidents
were mask related,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.760
the no-fly list would unfairly
target maskless passengers.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.150
The senators wrote "Creating
a federal no-fly list

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.810
for unruly passengers who
are skeptical of this mandate

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.870
would seemingly equate them to terrorists

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.710
who seek to actively take
the lives of Americans

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.320
and perpetrate attacks on the homeland."

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.820
- They also complain
that it equates people

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.210
who are objecting to the
mask mandate to terrorists,

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.780
which they say is unfair and overblown.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.370
That's not quite exactly
what Delta had proposed,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.680
it's not necessarily
that Delta had proposed

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.920
everyone who won't wear masks
should be banned from flying,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.890
but obviously it would be controversial.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.300
- [Reporter] But how
realistic is a no-fly list?

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.500
Now that the unruly
passenger rate has fallen

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.950
since its dramatic spike last year,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.473
what's more, mask mandates

00:05:26.473 --> 00:05:29.570
which were one of the biggest
points of friction last year,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.210
are coming to an end in March.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:34.750
- This is an issue that
has been so politicized,

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.430
that people who want to wear masks

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.800
and people who don't want to wear masks

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.360
are both in conflict with each other.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.180
And so sometimes the
aggressors on the planes

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.860
are the people who are so angry

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.840
that other people just
won't follow the rules.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.590
- [Reporter] The agency with the authority

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.380
to extend the mask mandate, the TSA,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.240
has said nothing thus far
about another extension.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.010
Last December, the TSA
extended its mask mandate

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.350
for another three months,

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:04.390
to combat a surge in
Omicron variant cases.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.140
(dramatic music)

